WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.013
Ladies and gentlemen, please welcome John Cho.

2
00:00:03.013 --> 00:00:05.909
(audience cheering)

3
00:00:05.909 --> 00:00:08.131
I'm not kidding, since day one I've been like,

4
00:00:08.131 --> 00:00:11.115
"Can you get John Cho to come on the show?"

5
00:00:11.115 --> 00:00:12.890
How are you?

6
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.721
My pleasure, thank you so much.

7
00:00:14.721 --> 00:00:17.262
Oh my goodness.
But it's true.

8
00:00:17.262 --> 00:00:18.770
Hi, everybody.

9
00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.230
I am one of your biggest fans.

10
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.580
This is nuts.

11
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.970
Now I went way back on my research with you.

12
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.780
Uh-oh.
And I cannot even

13
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:34.020
like handle the fact that you changed the world

14
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.020
when you said a certain line of dialogue in a certain film.

15
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.560
Will you tell me about that?

16
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.070
Oh, yes.

17
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.680
I introduced, and my apologies,

18
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.581
I introduced the word MILF into the vocabulary.

19
00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:50.990
(audience clapping)

20
00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.443
No apologies needed.

21
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.193
I was once young, yeah.

22
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.820
You don't age, you look so good.

23
00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:58.790
You just have different hair.

24
00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:00.630
Yeah.
That's all I see.

25
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.320
Did you know you when you said that line

26
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.790
that it would become an iconic catchphrase like forever?

27
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.670
It still is relevant now as it was then.

28
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.360
No idea, in fact, that's not the part I auditioned for.

29
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.660
I was kind of a featured extra in the rest of the movie

30
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.110
and, you know, we did a table read

31
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.360 line:15%
and I was one of the people who showed up.

32
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.420 line:15%
And they said, "Can you do these 10 parts," or whatever?

33
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.527
And the director said,

34
00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:28.070
"Would you like to do this other scene?"

35
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.610
And so it all seems really random,

36
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.560
but here we are that sort of,

37
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.393
I guess it launched my career.

38
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.490
But I'm so also excited

39
00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.440
to learn that you were a teacher?

40
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.590
Yeah, I had a piece of paper that said

41
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.370
I was an English major when I graduated from college,

42
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.080
and I couldn't get a job waiting tables.

43
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.080
So I saw this ad, and so I taught English Lit for a year

44
00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.490
to seventh and 10th graders.

45
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.350
What was that like?

46
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.220
It was very stressful.

47
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.560
It was an honor, it really was.

48
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.640
But I quit that year.

49
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.630
After that year I didn't go back to it,

50
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:18.630
because it's important to be selfish if you're an actor,

51
00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.150
and you can't be selfish if you're a teacher.

52
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.730
You have to live for your class,

53
00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.116
and I wasn't prepared to do that at that age.

54
00:02:27.116 --> 00:02:29.699 line:15%
(upbeat music)

